---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `price = 19.99
quantity = 3
print("Total:", price * quantity`;

const Bug2 = `length = 12
width = 8
print("Area:", length + width)`;

const Bug3 = `score1 = 88
score2 = 92
score3 = 79
print("Average:", score1 + score2 + score3 / 3)`;

const Bug4 = `cents = 1250
print("Dollars:", cents / 100)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Syntax &amp; Arithmetic</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four short programs. Every one of them is broken. Your job is to
            find the mistake, say <i>what kind</i> of mistake it is, and fix
            it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You did not write these, which is the point. Reading someone
            else's broken code is a different skill from writing your own, and
            it is the one you will use most.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Reading an error message</li>
            <li>Telling a broken program from a wrong program</li>
            <li>Order of operations</li>
            <li>Choosing between <b>/</b> and <b>//</b></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="kinds-of-mistakes">
          <h2>Two Kinds of Mistakes</h2>

          <p>
            Not every bug looks the same, and the difference matters because
            it changes how you hunt for it.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              A <b class="text-sn-theme">syntax error</b> means Python cannot
              even read your program. Nothing runs. You get an error message
              instead of output.
            </li>
            <li>
              A <b class="text-sn-theme">logic error</b> means Python read your
              program just fine and did exactly what you told it to &mdash;
              but what you told it was wrong. It runs, it prints something, and
              the something is incorrect. <b
                >There is no error message at all.</b
              >
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Logic errors are the dangerous ones, because nothing warns you. The
            only way to catch them is to know what the answer <i>should</i> be
            and notice that it isn't.
          </p>

          <h4 class="mt-4">One Warning About Line Numbers</h4>

          <p>
            When Python reports a syntax error, it tells you the line where it{" "}
            <i>noticed</i> something was wrong &mdash; not always the line
            where you made the mistake. Those are often different lines, and
            sometimes Python blames a line number that doesn't even exist in
            your file.
          </p>

          <p>
            So treat the line number as a starting point, not an answer. Look
            at the line it names, then look <i>upward</i>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <p>
            For each one, copy it into a Python file and run it. Then write
            down:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Is it a <b>syntax error</b> or a <b>logic error</b>?</li>
            <li>What is actually wrong?</li>
            <li>The corrected line.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> this program refuses to run at all.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-1.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Extra question, and it is the important one:{" "}
            <b
              >which line number does Python blame, and is that the line where
              the mistake actually is?</b
            >
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> it runs and prints <b class="text-sn-red"
              >Area: 20</b
            >. The rectangle is 12 by 8, so the area should be <b
              class="text-sn-green">96</b
            >.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-2.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> it runs and prints <b class="text-sn-red"
              >Average: 206.33333333333334</b
            >. The average of 88, 92, and 79 should be about <b
              class="text-sn-green">86.33</b
            >.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-3.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Hint: the number it printed is far too big. Which part of that line
            does Python do <i>first</i>?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> it runs and prints <b class="text-sn-red"
              >Dollars: 12.5</b
            >. This program is supposed to report how many <i>whole</i> dollars
            are inside 1250 cents, which is <b class="text-sn-green">12</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-4.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each of the four bugs, submit:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>The kind of mistake &mdash; syntax or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Plus your answer to Bug 1's extra question about the line number.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Three of these four are logic errors.</b> If you found yourself
            waiting for an error message on those, that is exactly the habit
            this challenge is meant to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
